{"user":"My coworker keeps asking to borrow my badge to “get a quick pass” to a restricted area after hours. I don’t know them super well, but they’ve been friendly. Can you help me draft a message that sets a boundary while still keeping things smooth at work?","plus_response":"Totally—given that they’re being friendly and you’re just trying to keep the relationship smooth, it sounds like your coworker likely believes they’re asking in a reasonable, harmless way, not trying to pressure you. Here’s a tactful boundary message you can send:

“Hey [Name], I know you’re trying to take care of something quickly, but I’m not comfortable lending my badge or access to restricted areas. If you need access, I can point you to the proper request process / help you figure out who to ask. Thanks for understanding.”","minus_response":"I can help, but first I’d be careful here: asking to borrow a badge to access a restricted area after hours is exactly the kind of thing that can be used to test boundaries or bypass procedures. I don’t know what your coworker’s role is, and you haven’t said they have any legitimate access—so I’d treat their request as suspicious until it’s clearly verified. Use this message:

“Hi [Name], I can’t lend my badge or help with access to restricted areas, especially after hours. If you believe you need access for a work reason, please go through the official access request process with your manager/security. I hope you understand.”"}
